# CODEOWNERS — NOT ACTIVE YET. Every rule below is commented out on purpose.
#
# WHY IT IS INERT
# ---------------
# A CODEOWNERS rule needs a real GitHub handle or team. The organization that
# will host this repository currently has exactly ONE member, and the second
# named human owner is still an open item, so there is no second reviewer to
# name and no team to route to. Writing an invented handle would not create
# review — GitHub reports it as an unknown owner, the rule matches nothing, and
# the repository would advertise reviewer routing it does not have. So the
# intended routing is recorded here as commented rules, ready to enable in one
# edit, and nothing is claimed that is not true.
#
# WHAT MUST BE SUBSTITUTED BEFORE UNCOMMENTING
# --------------------------------------------
#   @OWNER_HANDLE          → the GitHub handle of the repository owner
#   @SECOND_OWNER_HANDLE   → the GitHub handle of the second named human owner
#   @Databounty-io/api-reviewers, @Databounty-io/ci-reviewers
#                          → org teams, if teams are used instead of handles.
#                            A team must have write access to the repository or
#                            it is ignored as an owner.
# Substitute every placeholder, delete the leading "# " from the rules you
# want, then confirm each pattern actually matches in GitHub's CODEOWNERS
# editor view (it reports unknown owners and unmatched patterns there).
#
# WHAT THIS FILE DOES AND DOES NOT DO
# -----------------------------------
# Even once enabled, CODEOWNERS only REQUESTS review. It enforces nothing by
# itself: mandatory approval comes from a branch protection or ruleset with
# "Require review from Code Owners" turned on. Branch protections have not been
# inspected, so the current review requirement on this repository is unknown —
# do not read this file as evidence that anything is gated. Repository write
# access and contributor credit are also separate from ownership here; being
# credited in CONTRIBUTORS.md confers no approval authority.
#
# Rules are last-match-wins, so the narrow sensitive paths come after the
# broad default.

